/*
time complexity - O(n)
space complexity - O(n)
INTUITION
Use a deque to store indexes of elements in decreasing order of their values.
The front of the deque will always represent the maximum of the current window.

As you move the right pointer (r):

Remove elements from the back of the deque if they are smaller than the current number, because they can never be part of the maximum again.

Add the current index to the back.

Ensure the deque only contains indexes inside the current window:

If the left pointer (l) crosses the index at the front of the deque, remove it from the front.

Once a full window is formed (r ≥ k − 1):

The element at nums[deque.front] is the maximum of that window.

Store it in the result.

Then move the left pointer (l).

Continue sliding the window until reaching the end of nums.

*/






class Solution {
    public int[] maxSlidingWindow(int[] nums, int k) {
        Deque<Integer> d = new ArrayDeque();
        int[] ans = new int[nums.length - k+1 ];
        //store indexes rather than numbers to avoid conflict incase of duplicates
        int l=0,r=0;
        while(r<nums.length){
            while(!d.isEmpty() && nums[d.getLast()]<nums[r]){
                d.pollLast();
            }
            d.offerLast(r);
            if(l>d.getFirst())//check if current window l occurs after the max element in the deque
            {
                d.pollFirst();
            }
            if(r+1 >=k )//start recording ans when one full window is processed i.e r >=k-1
            {
                ans[l]=nums[d.getFirst()];
                l++;
            }
            
            r++;
        }
        return ans;
    }
}
